  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nBy Sunday, the upper level ridge mentioned in the short term \nforecast continues to push eastward and this shift will bring more \nmoisture to the eastern half of the CWA with better rain chances for \nthe Permian Basin and lower Trans Pecos. Monday sees drier \nconditions for a majority of the region, but a cold front pushes in \nfrom the north. Guidance continues to show the front just making to \nthe region during the day on Monday, but stalling out before pushing \nfurther south than the Permian Basin. Monday night sees the front \npush through and temperatures on Tuesday fall to near normal in the \n80s for most. \"Cooler\" temperatures last through the end of the \nweek with Big Bend remaining the hottest with temperatures in the \n90s. \n\nRain chances increase both with the front and as another batch of \nmoisture is pulled in from the west and southwest. Guidance \ncontinues to show the focus of moisture over New Mexico with a \ngradual decrease in favorable conditions from west to east across \nthe CWA. Medium to long range guidance hold 40-60% rain chances \nacross SE NM through the end of the week. Flooding will quickly \nbecome a concern with potential rounds of convection expected \neach day, should the forecast continue to hold. \n\n-Stickney",
